# Knowledge for whispers. E(aliases, summary, when/where, details..., legacy). Aliases are lowercase, separated by |.
E('ancient egypt|egyptians|pharaohs', 'Ancient Egypt was one of the world’s first great civilisations, built along the River Nile in north-east Africa.', 'It lasted roughly three thousand years, from the unification of Upper and Lower Egypt around 3100 BC until Rome took it over in 30 BC.',
  'Its rulers, the pharaohs, were seen as god-kings who stood between the gods and the people.', 'The Nile’s yearly flood laid down rich black soil, which let Egyptian farmers grow far more food than they needed.', 'Egyptians wrote in hieroglyphs, a script of pictures and sound-signs carved on temples and painted on papyrus.', 'They mummified their dead because they believed the body had to survive for the soul to live on in the afterlife.', 'History is usually split into the Old, Middle and New Kingdoms, with unstable “intermediate periods” in between.', 'Famous rulers include Khufu, Hatshepsut, Akhenaten, Tutankhamun, Ramesses II and, at the very end, Cleopatra VII.',
  'Egypt gave the world monumental architecture, an early 365-day calendar, advances in medicine and some of the oldest writing on Earth.')
E('pyramids|great pyramid|pyramid of giza|pyramids of giza|giza', 'The Great Pyramid of Giza is the largest of the three pyramids on the Giza plateau, near modern Cairo.', 'It was built for the pharaoh Khufu around 2560 BC, during Egypt’s Old Kingdom.',
  'It was originally about 146 metres tall and stayed the tallest human-made structure in the world for nearly four thousand years.', 'It is made of roughly 2.3 million stone blocks, many weighing several tonnes.', 'Most historians now think it was built by paid, organised work gangs, not slaves, who lived in a town nearby.', 'Its sides are lined up almost exactly with north, south, east and west.', 'It is the only one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World still standing.',
  'The pyramids remain the most famous symbol of ancient Egypt and a lasting puzzle about how early societies organised huge projects.')
E('tutankhamun|king tut|tutankhamen', 'Tutankhamun was a young pharaoh of Egypt’s 18th Dynasty, famous today mostly because his tomb was found almost intact.', 'He ruled from about 1332 to 1323 BC and died at around 19 years old.',
  'He came to the throne as a child, after the religious upheaval of Akhenaten, and his reign restored worship of the old gods.', 'The British archaeologist Howard Carter discovered his tomb in the Valley of the Kings in November 1922.', 'The tomb held more than five thousand objects, including his solid gold burial mask.', 'Stories of a “curse” spread after the expedition’s sponsor, Lord Carnarvon, died in 1923, but there is no evidence for one.', 'Studies of his mummy suggest he had a club foot and suffered from malaria.',
  'His tomb gave the modern world its clearest picture of royal burial in ancient Egypt and sparked a worldwide craze for Egyptology.')
E('cleopatra|cleopatra vii', 'Cleopatra VII was the last active ruler of Ptolemaic Egypt, a Greek-speaking dynasty founded after Alexander the Great.', 'She lived from 69 to 30 BC and ruled Egypt from 51 BC until her death.',
  'She was highly educated and was said to speak many languages, and she was the first of her dynasty to learn Egyptian.', 'She formed alliances, and relationships, with two of Rome’s most powerful men: Julius Caesar and then Mark Antony.', 'She had a son with Caesar, Caesarion, and three children with Antony.', 'Antony and Cleopatra were defeated by Octavian at the Battle of Actium in 31 BC.', 'She died in 30 BC, traditionally by the bite of an asp, and Egypt became a Roman province.',
  'Her life has been retold endlessly, from Shakespeare’s Antony and Cleopatra to Hollywood, often more as legend than history.')
E('ramesses ii|ramses ii|ramesses the great|ramses', 'Ramesses II, often called Ramesses the Great, was one of the most powerful and celebrated pharaohs of ancient Egypt.', 'He ruled for about 66 years, from around 1279 to 1213 BC, during the New Kingdom.',
  'He fought the Hittites at the Battle of Kadesh around 1274 BC, which he presented at home as a great victory.', 'Later, Egypt and the Hittites signed one of the earliest known peace treaties.', 'He built enormously, including the temples at Abu Simbel and the Ramesseum.', 'He is thought to have had more than a hundred children.', 'Later pharaohs called him “the Great Ancestor”.',
  'His monuments made him a byword for royal power, and he is often linked, without firm evidence, to the pharaoh of the Exodus story.')
E('mesopotamia|sumer|sumerians', 'Mesopotamia is the region between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers, mostly in modern Iraq, often called the cradle of civilisation.', 'Its first cities, like Uruk and Ur, grew up in the south, in Sumer, from around 4000 to 3000 BC.',
  'The Sumerians developed cuneiform, one of the world’s first writing systems, pressing wedge-shaped marks into clay tablets.', 'They built stepped temple towers called ziggurats.', 'Mesopotamians used a base-60 number system, which is why we still have 60 minutes in an hour and 360 degrees in a circle.', 'Later empires there included the Akkadians, Babylonians and Assyrians.', 'The Epic of Gilgamesh, one of the oldest surviving works of literature, comes from Mesopotamia.',
  'Writing, the wheel, early law codes and city life all have deep roots in Mesopotamia.')
E('hammurabi|code of hammurabi', 'Hammurabi was a king of Babylon best known for his code of laws.', 'He ruled from about 1792 to 1750 BC.',
  'He expanded Babylon from a small city-state into an empire covering most of Mesopotamia.', 'His code has 282 laws, carved on a tall black stone pillar now kept in the Louvre in Paris.', 'It covers trade, property, family life, wages and crime.', 'It is famous for the principle of “an eye for an eye”, though punishments depended on a person’s social class.', 'The stele shows Hammurabi receiving the laws from Shamash, the god of justice.',
  'It is one of the earliest and most complete written legal codes, and a landmark in the idea that laws should be public.')
E('epic of gilgamesh|gilgamesh', 'The Epic of Gilgamesh is an ancient Mesopotamian poem and one of the oldest surviving great works of literature.', 'Its earliest versions date from around 2100 BC, with the best-known version from about 1200 BC.',
  'It follows Gilgamesh, a proud king of Uruk, and his friend Enkidu.', 'After Enkidu dies, Gilgamesh goes on a quest to escape death.', 'It includes a flood story strikingly similar to that of Noah.', 'Much of it survives on clay tablets found in the library of the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal at Nineveh.',
  'It remains a powerful story about friendship, grief and accepting that we are mortal.')
E('ancient greece|greeks|ancient greeks|classical greece', 'Ancient Greece was a civilisation of independent city-states around the Aegean Sea, whose ideas shaped much of the Western world.', 'Its classical age is usually dated to the 5th and 4th centuries BC.',
  'The most famous city-states were Athens, a centre of democracy and philosophy, and Sparta, a militarised society.', 'The Greeks gave us the Olympic Games, theatre, history writing and much of Western philosophy.', 'They fought off two Persian invasions, at battles such as Marathon, Thermopylae and Salamis.', 'Athens and Sparta later fought each other in the long Peloponnesian War.', 'Greek culture spread across the Mediterranean and Middle East after Alexander the Great’s conquests.',
  'Democracy, geometry, drama, medicine and philosophy all owe a great deal to the ancient Greeks.')
E('athens|athenian democracy|democracy in athens', 'Athens was the leading city-state of classical Greece and the birthplace of democracy.', 'Its democracy began with the reforms of Cleisthenes around 508 BC.',
  'Adult male citizens could vote directly in the Assembly, though women, slaves and foreigners could not.', 'Many officials were chosen by lot rather than by election.', 'Under Pericles in the 5th century BC, Athens built the Parthenon on the Acropolis.', 'It was home to Socrates, Plato, Aristotle and the great playwrights.', 'Athens lost the Peloponnesian War to Sparta in 404 BC.',
  'The Athenian experiment in rule by citizens is still the starting point for discussions about democracy.')
E('sparta|spartans', 'Sparta was a powerful city-state in southern Greece, famous for its military discipline.', 'It was at its height between the 6th and 4th centuries BC.',
  'Spartan boys entered a harsh state training system called the agoge at around age seven.', 'Sparta depended on the labour of helots, a large enslaved population.', 'Spartan women had more freedom than women in most Greek cities, including owning property.', 'Three hundred Spartans under King Leonidas made a famous last stand at Thermopylae in 480 BC.', 'Sparta defeated Athens in the Peloponnesian War, but declined after losing to Thebes at Leuctra in 371 BC.',
  'The word “spartan”, meaning plain and tough, comes from their way of life.')
E('thermopylae|battle of thermopylae|300 spartans|leonidas', 'The Battle of Thermopylae was a famous last stand by a small Greek force against the invading Persian army.', 'It was fought in 480 BC at a narrow coastal pass in central Greece.',
  'King Leonidas of Sparta led about seven thousand Greeks, including his three hundred Spartans.', 'The Persian army of Xerxes I was vastly larger.', 'The Greeks held the pass for three days until a local man showed the Persians a path around it.', 'Leonidas sent most of the army away and stayed to fight with his Spartans and a few allies, who were killed.', 'Weeks later the Greek navy beat the Persians at Salamis.',
  'Thermopylae became a lasting symbol of courage against overwhelming odds.')
E('alexander the great|alexander of macedon|alexander iii of macedon', 'Alexander the Great was a king of Macedon who built one of the largest empires of the ancient world.', 'He lived from 356 to 323 BC and became king at 20, after his father Philip II was murdered.',
  'He was tutored as a boy by the philosopher Aristotle.', 'He defeated the Persian Empire in a series of battles, including Issus and Gaugamela.', 'His empire stretched from Greece and Egypt to what is now Pakistan.', 'He founded many cities, the most famous being Alexandria in Egypt.', 'He never lost a battle, but his troops refused to go further when they reached India.', 'He died in Babylon at 32, possibly of illness, and his generals split the empire between them.',
  'His conquests spread Greek language and culture across the Middle East, beginning the Hellenistic age.')
E('socrates', 'Socrates was an Athenian philosopher, one of the founders of Western philosophy.', 'He lived from about 470 to 399 BC.',
  'He wrote nothing himself; we know him mostly through his student Plato.', 'He questioned people relentlessly to expose weak reasoning, a style now called the Socratic method.', 'He is famous for saying he was wise only because he knew that he knew nothing.', 'In 399 BC Athens put him on trial for “corrupting the youth” and not honouring the city’s gods.', 'He was sentenced to death and drank poison hemlock rather than flee.',
  'His idea that “the unexamined life is not worth living” still sits at the heart of philosophy.')
E('plato', 'Plato was an Athenian philosopher, a student of Socrates and the teacher of Aristotle.', 'He lived from about 428 to 348 BC.',
  'He wrote dialogues, usually starring Socrates, that discuss justice, love, knowledge and the soul.', 'His best-known work, The Republic, imagines an ideal state ruled by philosopher-kings.', 'His “allegory of the cave” compares most people to prisoners mistaking shadows for reality.', 'He founded the Academy in Athens, often called the first university in the Western world.', 'His theory of Forms says that perfect, unchanging ideas lie behind the imperfect things we see.',
  'The philosopher Alfred North Whitehead said all of Western philosophy is a series of footnotes to Plato.')
E('aristotle', 'Aristotle was a Greek philosopher and scientist, a student of Plato and tutor to Alexander the Great.', 'He lived from 384 to 322 BC.',
  'He wrote on an astonishing range of subjects: logic, ethics, politics, biology, physics, poetry and more.', 'He founded his own school in Athens, the Lyceum.', 'He developed formal logic, including the syllogism.', 'In biology he carefully observed and classified hundreds of animals.', 'His ethics focus on virtue and living a flourishing life, which he called eudaimonia.',
  'His ideas dominated European and Islamic thought for nearly two thousand years.')
E('pythagoras|pythagorean theorem', 'Pythagoras was a Greek philosopher and mathematician, best known for the theorem named after him.', 'He lived from about 570 to 495 BC and founded a community in southern Italy.',
  'The Pythagorean theorem says that in a right-angled triangle, a² + b² = c², where c is the longest side.', 'The relationship was actually known earlier in Babylon and India, but his school is credited with proving it.', 'His followers believed numbers were the key to understanding the universe.', 'They also linked mathematics to music, discovering that harmonious notes follow simple number ratios.', 'His community had strict rules, including, famously, not eating beans.',
  'The theorem is still one of the first proofs students learn, and it underpins geometry, navigation and engineering.')
E('archimedes', 'Archimedes was a Greek mathematician, engineer and inventor from Syracuse in Sicily.', 'He lived from about 287 to 212 BC.',
  'According to legend, he shouted “Eureka!” in his bath when he realised how to measure volume by displacement.', 'He worked out the principle of buoyancy, now called Archimedes’ principle.', 'He estimated the value of pi with remarkable accuracy.', 'He explained the lever, reportedly saying, “Give me a place to stand and I will move the Earth.”', 'He designed war machines to defend Syracuse against Rome and was killed when the city fell.',
  'He is often ranked among the greatest mathematicians of all time.')
E('homer|iliad|odyssey|the iliad|the odyssey', 'Homer is the legendary Greek poet credited with the Iliad and the Odyssey, the oldest works of Western literature.', 'The poems are thought to date from the 8th or 7th century BC.',
  'The Iliad tells of the anger of Achilles during the Trojan War.', 'The Odyssey follows Odysseus on his ten-year journey home from Troy.', 'They were composed for oral performance and passed down by singers before being written down.', 'Scholars still debate whether Homer was one person, several people or a tradition.',
  'Phrases like “Achilles’ heel”, “Trojan horse” and “odyssey” all come from these stories.')
E('trojan war|troy|trojan horse', 'The Trojan War is the legendary war between the Greeks and the city of Troy, told in Homer’s Iliad and later poems.', 'If it happened, it would date to around the 12th or 13th century BC.',
  'In legend it began when the Trojan prince Paris took Helen, wife of King Menelaus of Sparta.', 'The Greeks besieged Troy for ten years.', 'They finally won by hiding soldiers inside a giant wooden horse, which the Trojans pulled into the city.', 'Archaeologists have found the remains of a real city, Hisarlik in modern Turkey, that matches Troy.',
  'Whether or not it happened as told, it became one of the founding stories of Western culture.')
E('olympic games|olympics|ancient olympics', 'The Olympic Games began in ancient Greece as a religious festival in honour of Zeus.', 'The traditional date of the first Games is 776 BC, at Olympia.',
  'They were held every four years, a period the Greeks called an Olympiad.', 'Events included running, wrestling, boxing, chariot racing and the pentathlon.', 'Winners received an olive wreath, though their home cities often rewarded them richly.', 'The ancient Games ended around AD 393.', 'The Frenchman Pierre de Coubertin revived them, and the first modern Olympics were held in Athens in 1896.',
  'Today the Olympics are the world’s largest international sporting event.')
